// 14758
[tns_room1_box1_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> The day we moved here, Primrose was two. I remember her toddling next to us, carrying a book, one of the bigger ones.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> You couldn't stop her. We called her our little Pack Yak Primrose.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Sure, she slowed us down, made the journey painfully long, but you try to tell her to stop!
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Ah. Good days.
default=null

// 14759
[tns_room1_box1_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=310>The day we moved here, Primrose was two. I remember her toddling next to us, carrying a book, one of the bigger ones.
val=1,<p=310>You couldn't stop her. We called her our little Pack Yak Primrose.
val=2,<p=310>Sure, she slowed us down, made the journey painfully long, but you try to tell her to stop!
val=3,<p=310>Ah. Good days.
default=null

// 14760
[tns_room1_box2_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> You can ignore death, blood, murder. The mind's flexible like that. They all happen to far away people with far away lives.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It was blissful. We lived in a house where those things didn't exist.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> But it was like we were pulling back the curtains and hoping for the best. Hoping that those flimsy pieces of fabric would keep the violence out.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> We were wrong. The world got in. A sea troll got in...
default=null

// 14761
[tns_room1_box2_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>You can ignore death, blood, murder. The mind's flexible like that. They all happen to far away people with far away lives.
val=1,<p=2>It was blissful. We lived in a house where those things didn't exist.
val=2,<p=2>But it was like we were pulling back the curtains and hoping for the best. Hoping that those flimsy pieces of fabric would keep the violence out.
val=3,<p=0>We were wrong. The world got in. A sea troll got in...
default=null

// 14762
[tns_room1_box3_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Gail didn't come to us at first. She'd been watching us for some time from the Needle, making sure we weren't monsters, I guess.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I mean, we'd seen her. She wasn't great at hiding herself, after all. She's ten-foot tall and blue.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Joss assumed the worst, as he always did. Tried to scare her away with air blasts. Nothing that would hurt her, just to send a message.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> She kept clear. It was only when Joss left - no, abandoned - us that she felt courage enough to talk to me.
default=null

// 14763
[tns_room1_box3_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>Gail didn't come to us at first. She'd been watching us for some time from the Needle, making sure we weren't monsters, I guess.
val=1,<p=2>I mean, we'd seen her. She wasn't great at hiding herself, after all. She's ten-foot tall and blue.
val=2,<p=2>Joss assumed the worst, as he always did. Tried to scare her away with air blasts. Nothing that would hurt her, just to send a message.
val=3,<p=2>She kept clear. It was only when Joss left - no, abandoned - us that she felt courage enough to talk to me.
default=null

// 14764
[tns_room1_box4_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose is so strong, but people can't see past the illness.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Doctors, visitors, they all make that mistake. Judge her by the disease, the microscopic evil magnified into something huge.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> But I see the strength. Damn girl never lets an argument go un-won. She'll fight for the things she loves, for sure.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> And she's full of love. Not always for her mum, but, you know, it's there. Growing up, she loved everything, cared for everything.
default=null

// 14765
[tns_room1_box4_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=300>Primrose is so strong, but people can't see past the illness.
val=1,<p=300>Doctors, visitors, they all make that mistake. Judge her by the disease, the microscopic evil magnified into something huge.
val=2,<p=310>But I see the strength. Damn girl never lets an argument go un-won. She'll fight for the things she loves, for sure.
val=3,<p=310>And she's full of love. Not always for her mum, but, you know, it's there. Growing up, she loved everything, cared for everything.
default=null

// 14766
[tns_room1_box5_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> My whole life, I've been surrounded by books. Not once have I read one.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I can see my tombstone: 'crushed under a pile of books'.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> When I met Joss, he was hidden behind a stack of wizarding tomes. He'd line our house with them.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose treated them as toys, then things to climb, then adventures to climb into.
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Her imagination was always wild. She'd play out the God Wars with Saradomin as a ladle and Zamorak as a pepper pot.
default=null

// 14767
[tns_room1_box5_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>My whole life, I've been surrounded by books. Not once have I read one.
val=1,<p=315>I can see my tombstone: 'crushed under a pile of books'.
val=2,<p=2>When I met Joss, he was hidden behind a stack of wizarding tomes. He'd line our house with them.
val=3,<p=2>Primrose treated them as toys, then things to climb, then adventures to climb into.
val=4,<p=2>Her imagination was always wild. She'd play out the God Wars with Saradomin as a ladle and Zamorak as a pepper pot.
default=null

// 14768
[tns_room1_box6_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Every weekend, it was the same thing. Pack provisions, wrap some books in blessed cloth and worry about leaving Primrose behind.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Then it was the trip to the Wizards' Tower, cart and all. Sell some books to Sloop, ask around about Joss, cook a Weekend Banquet for the elite wizards.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Then it was back home, worried about Primrose and whether Gail had kept her safe.
default=null

// 14769
[tns_room1_box6_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>Every weekend, it was the same thing. Pack provisions, wrap some books in blessed cloth and worry about leaving Primrose behind.
val=1,<p=2>Then it was the trip to the Wizards' Tower, cart and all. Sell some books to Sloop, ask around about Joss, cook a Weekend Banquet for the elite wizards.
val=2,<p=2>Then it was back home, worried about Primrose and whether Gail had kept her safe.
default=null

// 14770
[tns_room1_box7_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Some memories stick in you like barbs. They hurt whenever you accidentally catch yourself on them.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I...I was on the way back from the Wizards' Tower. The day had gone well: I'd sold some books for more than I expected, and I'd gotten the cooking job.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> But I arrived at the house. There was the bang-bang of the door, slamming in the wind.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I abandoned the cart, rushed in. I called for Primrose. Nothing.
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> A stray sea troll had found its way into the house. It was long gone when I got there, but... It...
val=5,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It had killed her. Primrose.
val=6,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> That was the first time Gail talked to me. I couldn't move, I was crying on the floor of the kitchen. And Gail came to me.
default=null

// 14771
[tns_room1_box7_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=309>Some memories stick in you like barbs. They hurt whenever you accidentally catch yourself on them.
val=1,<p=2>I...I was on the way back from the Wizards' Tower. The day had gone well: I'd sold some books for more than I expected, and I'd gotten the cooking job.
val=2,<p=302>But I arrived at the house. There was the bang-bang of the door, slamming in the wind.
val=3,<p=302>I abandoned the cart, rushed in. I called for Primrose. Nothing.
val=4,<p=302>A stray sea troll had found its way into the house. It was long gone when I got there, but... It...
val=5,<p=309>It had killed her. Primrose.
val=6,<p=309>That was the first time Gail talked to me. I couldn't move, I was crying on the floor of the kitchen. And Gail came to me.
default=null

// 14772
[tns_room1_box8_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> 'Temper-temper'. That's what Joss would always say to me. Gods, it would only make me angrier.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Whenever I would hook his nose out of a damned book: 'temper-temper'.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Whenever I called him on his laziness, doing the chores with some cheap magic: 'temper-temper'.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> They were even his last words to me before he left. Wherever the bastard has gone, I imagine him repeating them like a cheap toy.
default=null

// 14773
[tns_room1_box8_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=300>'Temper-temper'. That's what Joss would always say to me. Gods, it would only make me angrier.
val=1,<p=300>Whenever I would hook his nose out of a damned book: 'temper-temper'.
val=2,<p=300>Whenever I called him on his laziness, doing the chores with some cheap magic: 'temper-temper'.
val=3,<p=300>They were even his last words to me before he left. Wherever the bastard has gone, I imagine him repeating them like a cheap toy.
default=null

// 14774
[tns_room1_box9_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> A year to live. That's all the doctor could give her, holding his hands out upright like a pauper. 'Please miss, this is all I can give you'.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose just half-smiled when the doctor told her. I was so angry at her for brushing it all aside.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I needed her to cry, break down, something. Just react, damn it!
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> *sighs*
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Each month, I watched helplessly as she got thinner. Her skin became translucent. But you know Primrose: it just meant that her character shone through brighter.
val=5,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I just wish...I wish she didn't hide that character away. So often, she reserved it for her creatures. Her monsters.
default=null

// 14775
[tns_room1_box9_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=300>A year to live. That's all the doctor could give her, holding his hands out upright like a pauper. 'Please miss, this is all I can give you'.
val=1,<p=300>Primrose just half-smiled when the doctor told her. I was so angry at her for brushing it all aside.
val=2,<p=300>I needed her to cry, break down, something. Just react, damn it!
val=3,<p=0>*sighs*
val=4,<p=0>Each month, I watched helplessly as she got thinner. Her skin became translucent. But you know Primrose: it just meant that her character shone through brighter.
val=5,<p=0>I just wish...I wish she didn't hide that character away. So often, she reserved it for her creatures. Her monsters.
default=null

// 14776
[tns_room1_box10_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> When I was younger, I'd fall in love twice a week. It would come to me as easy as a temper, and would disappear just as quick.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I thought I would fall out of love with Joss as quickly as the others. In the end, the love went, but it took a while.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> There was my love for Saradomin, of course. But I wasn't getting love in return. The score wasn't equal.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose has always been the exception. To fall out of love with Primrose would be to fall out of love with, well, everything.
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I know what you're thinking. No - that time doesn't count. I wasn't angry with Primrose. I was angry at THEM.
default=null

// 14777
[tns_room1_box10_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=310>When I was younger, I'd fall in love twice a week. It would come to me as easy as a temper, and would disappear just as quick.
val=1,<p=2>I thought I would fall out of love with Joss as quickly as the others. In the end, the love went, but it took a while.
val=2,<p=2>There was my love for Saradomin, of course. But I wasn't getting love in return. The score wasn't equal.
val=3,<p=310>Primrose has always been the exception. To fall out of love with Primrose would be to fall out of love with, well, everything.
val=4,<p=300>I know what you're thinking. No - that time doesn't count. I wasn't angry with Primrose. I was angry at THEM.
default=null

// 14778
[tns_room1_box11_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> There was a day when the illness hit her hard. A day when she couldn't ignore it.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose dropped her favourite book: the Adventures of Hannibus, I think it was. She couldn't pick it up. It was open on its back page, the ending staring right up at her.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It was the first time I'd seen her cry since the illness. I tried to hand it to her, but she shoved it away. I felt like her jailer.
default=null

// 14779
[tns_room1_box11_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=0>There was a day when the illness hit her hard. A day when she couldn't ignore it.
val=1,<p=0>Primrose dropped her favourite book: the Adventures of Hannibus, I think it was. She couldn't pick it up. It was open on its back page, the ending staring right up at her.
val=2,<p=0>It was the first time I'd seen her cry since the illness. I tried to hand it to her, but she shoved it away. I felt like her jailer.
default=null

// 14780
[tns_room1_box12_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Is there anyone more self-involved than a wizard?
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I get it, I do. As a wizard, you have to study for years. You have to find a niche that no wizard has found. You have to fight over whatever resources there are.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> But none of them - not Joss, not Sloop, not the Wizards' Tower - can see how self-centred they are. The Tower's just a chocolate box of egos, all in a variety of flavours.
default=null

// 14781
[tns_room1_box12_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>Is there anyone more self-involved than a wizard?
val=1,<p=2>I get it, I do. As a wizard, you have to study for years. You have to find a niche that no wizard has found. You have to fight over whatever resources there are.
val=2,<p=2>But none of them - not Joss, not Sloop, not the Wizards' Tower - can see how self-centred they are. The Tower's just a chocolate box of egos, all in a variety of flavours.
default=null

// 14782
[tns_room1_box13_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I guess we should have stopped it earlier? Even when she was young, she'd have imaginary friend after imaginary friend.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> They'd even have relatives! Imaginary sisters and brothers would come to visit. Some would get lost, and we'd have to pretend to look for them.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It got serious once Primrose got ill. She would keep their counsel, rather than talk to me.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> She did it when I wasn't watching, but I knew. She would write poems with them, sing with them, confide in them, ask them for their thoughts.
default=null

// 14783
[tns_room1_box13_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>I guess we should have stopped it earlier? Even when she was young, she'd have imaginary friend after imaginary friend.
val=1,<p=2>They'd even have relatives! Imaginary sisters and brothers would come to visit. Some would get lost, and we'd have to pretend to look for them.
val=2,<p=308>It got serious once Primrose got ill. She would keep their counsel, rather than talk to me.
val=3,<p=0>She did it when I wasn't watching, but I knew. She would write poems with them, sing with them, confide in them, ask them for their thoughts.
default=null

// 14784
[tns_room1_box14_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Saradomin. This is my final prayer.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> If you're listening, then damn you. You choose to listen now? You choose the moment when I give this up? Damn you back to the Third Age.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> You know what? I don't care whether you listen or not. I've found someone else to have faith in.
default=null

// 14785
[tns_room1_box14_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=300>Saradomin. This is my final prayer.
val=1,<p=300>If you're listening, then damn you. You choose to listen now? You choose the moment when I give this up? Damn you back to the Third Age.
val=2,<p=300>You know what? I don't care whether you listen or not. I've found someone else to have faith in.
default=null

// 14786
[tns_room1_box15_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Sloop was my husband's apprentice. He was a boy, not much older than Primrose, I guess. But he'd caught the wizarding bug: self-centred and ambitious.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Still, he kept an ear out for news about Joss. Plus he bought my books - as often as he could spare the money. I ferried them in small batches, as he could only buy so many.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> You could tell he never found it easy talking to me. He wanted to keep our meetings as quick as possible. The boy has a dark secret, you can be sure of it.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Last trip, he asked for the Adventures of Hannibus, and said he would pay well.
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It's Primrose's favourite book. I...I don't know if I can take it from her. But we need to eat. We need her medicines.
default=null

// 14787
[tns_room1_box15_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=0>Sloop was my husband's apprentice. He was a boy, not much older than Primrose, I guess. But he'd caught the wizarding bug: self-centred and ambitious.
val=1,<p=0>Still, he kept an ear out for news about Joss. Plus he bought my books - as often as he could spare the money. I ferried them in small batches, as he could only buy so many.
val=2,<p=0>You could tell he never found it easy talking to me. He wanted to keep our meetings as quick as possible. The boy has a dark secret, you can be sure of it.
val=3,<p=0>Last trip, he asked for the Adventures of Hannibus, and said he would pay well.
val=4,<p=0>It's Primrose's favourite book. I...I don't know if I can take it from her. But we need to eat. We need her medicines.
default=null

// 14788
[tns_room1_box16_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> You've got to remember, up to this point Gail was a boogeyman. Someone who watched us and then sped away into the night.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Then she's in my house, talking to me, offering me wishes like I was a Princess from one of Primrose's books.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Gail led me to the hilltop. Her hands were so cold and hard. I felt numb, but I couldn't figure out if it was from me or her.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> She showed me something called the Needle. Gail asked again, did I really want my wish? She had to shake me to get an answer.
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> The Needle would take us both back 30 days. While Gail and I would remember what had happened, no one else would. We'd be the only ones to remember the sea troll's attack.
val=5,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> But Primrose would be alive.
val=6,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I agreed, and Gail pulled thread from the Needle. She separated the thread into strands, and a dome of fabric fell about us.
val=7,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> The dome flashed with blinding light. You couldn't see about you, it was impossible. As the light faded, Gail gestured me to my house and said 'Be with her'.
val=8,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I rushed through the door and checked the calendar. 30 days back - my daughter's birthday. I checked my bedroom. There wasn't another Megan in there.
val=9,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Then I went to Primrose. She was asleep in her bed. I sat in the chair, watching her. No one was going to budge me from that spot. Not ever.
default=null

// 14789
[tns_room1_box16_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>You've got to remember, up to this point Gail was a boogeyman. Someone who watched us and then sped away into the night.
val=1,<p=2>Then she's in my house, talking to me, offering me wishes like I was a Princess from one of Primrose's books.
val=2,<p=2>Gail led me to the hilltop. Her hands were so cold and hard. I felt numb, but I couldn't figure out if it was from me or her.
val=3,<p=2>She showed me something called the Needle. Gail asked again, did I really want my wish? She had to shake me to get an answer.
val=4,<p=2>The Needle would take us both back 30 days. While Gail and I would remember what had happened, no one else would. We'd be the only ones to remember the sea troll's attack.
val=5,<p=310>But Primrose would be alive.
val=6,<p=2>I agreed, and Gail pulled thread from the Needle. She separated the thread into strands, and a dome of fabric fell about us.
val=7,<p=2>The dome flashed with blinding light. You couldn't see about you, it was impossible. As the light faded, Gail gestured me to my house and said 'Be with her'.
val=8,<p=310>I rushed through the door and checked the calendar. 30 days back - my daughter's birthday. I checked my bedroom. There wasn't another Megan in there.
val=9,<p=310>Then I went to Primrose. She was asleep in her bed. I sat in the chair, watching her. No one was going to budge me from that spot. Not ever.
default=null

// 14790
[tns_room1_box17_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> The potions would strengthen her for a day or two, then she would be back in bed, just as ill.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It's like the disease was a bull, crashing through any barrier we put in front of it.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> But I couldn't help buying potion after potion. Near half of the money we had, it was spent on these little moments of strength.
default=null

// 14791
[tns_room1_box17_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=0>The potions would strengthen her for a day or two, then she would be back in bed, just as ill. It's like the disease was a bull, crashing through any barrier we put in front of it.
val=1,<p=0>But I couldn't help buying potion after potion. Near half of the money we had, it was spent on these little moments of strength.
default=null

// 14792
[tns_room1_box18_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I had no choice. I had to sell The Adventures of Hannibus. It was going to bring us food, medicine - which would make her strong again. We couldn't afford not to.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I was going to ask permission from Primrose. But she wouldn't have understood.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I took it from her as she slept. She had her arms around it, like she knew the plan. It took some jostling, but I had it. I can be delicate when I need to be.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> She didn't mention Hannibus the next morning. Not a word.
default=null

// 14793
[tns_room1_box18_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=0>I had no choice. I had to sell The Adventures of Hannibus. It was going to bring us food, medicine - which would make her strong again. We couldn't afford not to.
val=1,<p=2>I was going to ask permission from Primrose. But she wouldn't have understood.
val=2,<p=2>I took it from her as she slept. She had her arms around it, like she knew the plan. It took some jostling, but I had it. I can be delicate when I need to be.
val=3,<p=2>She didn't mention Hannibus the next morning. Not a word.
default=null

// 14794
[tns_room1_box19_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose always had a lovely singing voice.
default=null

// 14795
[tns_room1_box19_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=0>Primrose always had a lovely singing voice.
default=null

// 14796
[tns_room1_box20_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I opened the book on the way to selling it. I couldn't tell you why - I never had an impulse to read a book before.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I guess...I guess I was interested in why Primrose had so much love for it.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Inside was a slip of paper. A poem, lightly scrawled in feeble handwriting. Primrose confessed her love. She begged to be saved.
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> The world fell away from me. I stopped the cart, and turned around. My head thrummed with rage. I worked the horse near to death.
val=4,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> The fury was wild and directionless. I burned with rage at myself, then the flames moved to Joss. After all, everything bad came from that damned man.
val=5,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Finally, the fury rested on the books.
val=6,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> When I returned home, I threw them all into the fireplace. Primrose screeched from her bed, begging me to stop.
val=7,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I lit the fireplace, and the books burned. But the magic on the books was fuel, and flame mushroomed out of the pit, knocking me to the floor.
val=8,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I was scarred and drowsy. Primrose was screaming about her friends burning, over and over.
val=9,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Gail found me, probably thanks to Primrose. She applied some kind of salve to me, but there was no removing the scar. It was to be my brand.
default=null

// 14797
[tns_room1_box20_text_npc]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<p=2>I opened the book on the way to selling it. I couldn't tell you why - I never had an impulse to read a book before. I guess...I guess I was interested in why Primrose had so much love for it.
val=1,<p=2>Inside was a slip of paper. A poem, lightly scrawled in feeble handwriting. Primrose confessed her love. She begged to be saved.
val=2,<p=300>The world fell away from me. I stopped the cart, and turned around. My head thrummed with rage. I worked the horse near to death.
val=3,<p=300>The fury was wild and directionless. I burned with rage at myself, then the flames moved to Joss. After all, everything bad came from that damned man.
val=4,<p=300>Finally, the fury rested on the books.
val=5,<p=300>When I returned home, I threw them all into the fireplace. Primrose screeched from her bed, begging me to stop.
val=6,<p=300>I lit the fireplace, and the books burned. But the magic on the books was fuel, and flame mushroomed out of the pit, knocking me to the floor.
val=7,<p=300>I was scarred and drowsy. Primrose was screaming about her friends burning, over and over.
val=8,<p=300>Gail found me, probably thanks to Primrose. She applied some kind of salve to me, but there was no removing the scar. It was to be my brand.
default=null

// 14798
[tns_room1_box21_text]
inputtype=int
outputtype=string
val=0,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> It was the morning after I traveled back in time. 30 days. Back to the morning of Primrose's birthday.
val=1,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Primrose looked so much better than I remembered.
val=2,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> Here she was, unmarked by any sea troll, happy and soulful. She was alive, dear Gods. And happy!
val=3,<col=ff0000>Megan:</col> I resolved, in that moment, to never let her get hurt again. Gail, wonderful Gail, offered to keep an eye on Primrose while I was away.
default=null

// 14799
